---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import lab from "../data/lab.json";

const panelSize = Number(lab.summary.panel_size || 1000).toLocaleString("en-US");
---
<Layout
  title="How we count — Crawl Policy Index"
  description="Plain-language explanation of what the Crawl Policy Index measures, and what it does not."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">How we count</p>
    <h1>What these numbers mean — and what they don’t.</h1>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      This index records what websites <strong>say</strong> about AI crawlers in a public
      file called robots.txt. It is not a measure of traffic, and it is not a scrape of
      page content.
    </p>

    <h2>In one minute</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>We look at a fixed list of <span data-panel-size>{panelSize}</span> websites (this panel, not “the web”).</li>
      <li>Once a day we fetch each site’s public crawl rules.</li>
      <li>We record whether the site named a bot such as GPTBot or ClaudeBot, and whether it allowed, blocked, or mixed that bot.</li>
      <li>If the site never named the bot but said “block all bots,” we still count it — and we label it as that blanket policy, not as a named decision.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What is robots.txt?</h2>
    <p>
      It is a short public file most websites already publish. Search engines have used it
      for years. It is the site speaking in public: “this automated visitor may look around,
      that one may not.” Anyone can read it. We do.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do <strong>not</strong> open articles, product pages, or comment threads. We also
      do not yet measure paywalls or HTTP “pay to crawl” responses. Those are a different
      kind of rule and are out of scope here.
    </p>

    <h2>Named vs a blanket “no”</h2>
    <p>
      Many sites have one rule that says “block everyone.” That is a real policy, and we
      include it. We do <strong>not</strong> treat it as the site naming GPTBot or ClaudeBot.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the overview chart, pink is “named this bot and blocked it.” Purple is “never
      named this bot, but blocked all bots.” Both mean GPTBot cannot crawl. They are
      different kinds of decision.
    </p>
    <p>
      A site with no readable rules file is left out of the count. We do not treat silence
      as permission.
    </p>

    <h2>The file we were served — not Google’s copy</h2>
    <p>
      We fetch as ourselves: <strong>CrawlPolicyIndex/1.0</strong>, from our published
      address, once a day. We record the robots.txt that server sent <em>us</em>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some sites send a different file to crawlers they have verified by IP — Googlebot
      is the usual example — and a stricter fallback to everyone else. We do not pretend
      to be Googlebot. We do not guess the private copy. A site can block us, and every
      unverified bot, while still appearing in Google. That is not a contradiction in
      these tables. It is a two-channel policy, and we only see one channel.
    </p>
    <p>
      So “blocked GPTBot” here means: <strong>the public rules file we were given named
      GPTBot and said no</strong> — or said “block all bots.” It does not mean we watched
      GPTBot being refused at the door.
    </p>

    <h2>Why you should not write “the web”</h2>
    <p>
      These figures come from a frozen list of <span data-panel-size>{panelSize}</span>
      well-known sites (pinned Tranco list, snapshot {lab.generated_at.slice(0, 10)}).
      That is a defined panel, not a sample of every website. Do not write a percentage
      of the internet.
    </p>
    <p>
      Names in the tables are tokens we checked against each operator’s own documentation.
      Tokens we could not confirm are stored, but they are not in published figures.
    </p>

    <h2>Training, search, and a person asking</h2>
    <p>Companies often run more than one bot. We keep them apart on purpose:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Training AI</strong> — collecting pages to build a model (GPTBot, ClaudeBot).</li>
      <li><strong>AI search</strong> — collecting pages to answer questions or cite sources.</li>
      <li><strong>When a person asks</strong> — fetching one page because a human requested that link.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A publisher can block training and still allow search. Mixing those two into “AI bots”
      hides the story.
    </p>

    <h2>Switches that are not crawlers</h2>
    <p>
      Names such as Google-Extended and Applebot-Extended are opt-out signals. They do not
      crawl. Never add them into a “how many crawlers were blocked” total.
    </p>

    <h2>Googlebot is a sanity check</h2>
    <p>
      Almost no serious site wants to disappear from Google. If our tables showed a large
      share naming Googlebot and blocking it, first ask whether we are looking at the
      public fallback file — the one served to unverified visitors — not whether Google
      itself is locked out. A jump in that number is a reason to inspect the files, not
      to write “the web blocked Google.”
    </p>

    <h2>Keeping the record honest</h2>
    <p>
      We store the original file so we can re-read it if our software is wrong. A mistake
      is fixed by reading the same files again — not by quietly changing yesterday’s number.
      Every figure on the site should be explainable from this page. If it is not, do not cite it.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
